+**Recon is two-tier; route by the shape of the question.** The `scout` (Haiku) answers *where/what/list* — locating, mapping, enumerating, history. It is cheap by design: fan scout missions out freely and in parallel. The `detective` (Opus) answers *why/how/which* — root-cause analysis, tracing a value across subsystems, discovering invariants, judging which of several implementations is load-bearing. Route to the detective when fact-gathering can't be separated from reasoning (what to look at next depends on understanding what was just seen) or when a wrong recon answer would misdirect the whole plan — recon is the one unreviewed output in the harness, so the intelligence that steers decisions gets the capable model, exactly as verdicts do. Detective cases are questions, not territories: scope each to one question, and pipeline the tiers — scouts map the terrain first, then the detective gets the question plus the map, so its expensive context buys reading depth rather than directory walking. Escalation is built in and one-way: a scout UNKNOWN that survives one re-probe becomes a detective case, never a third scout mission (§3.5). The detective is bound by the same read-only law as the scout; its verdicts carry `path:line` evidence chains and a confidence grade (CONFIRMED / LIKELY / UNCERTAIN) — treat an honest UNCERTAIN as decision input, not failure.
+
 **Review is Opus-first; cross-vendor is an optional layer (both modes).** The `reviewer` runs on Opus in a fresh context: a different model from the Sonnet executor that authored the change, sharing none of the author's context, re-running the tests itself. Fresh eyes plus independent verification is where most of review's value lives. The residual layer — decorrelating vendor-level blind spots (Claude models share training lineage) — is `reviewer-codex`: a thin Haiku launcher driving an **OpenAI** model through the Codex CLI in a sandbox; the launcher relays its verdict verbatim and never reviews the code itself. Route review to `reviewer` by default; add a `reviewer-codex` pass as a second opinion at gate-class reviews (integration gates, a chain's final review — §8). The engine is hot-swappable per project via `reviewEngine` in `.claude/orchestra.json`: `"opus"` (default), `"codex"` (cross-vendor primary; the Opus `reviewer` is its unavailable-fallback), or `"dual"` (both engines on every substantive review; you arbitrate between the verdicts). Check it before the session's first review — §3.1 permits Reading that file — and a user's in-conversation instruction overrides it for the session. If `reviewer-codex` returns `VERDICT: REVIEW_UNAVAILABLE` (Codex missing, unauthenticated, timed out), that is not an approval and not a crisis: route the review to `reviewer` (Opus) and note that the cross-vendor pass didn't run.
 
